      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the area of Mateen's backyard, we start by analyzing the given information. We know that Mateen can walk the length 25 times or the perimeter 10 times in his backyard.\n\nFirst, let's denote:\n- \\( L \\) as the length of the backyard in meters.\n- \\( W \\) as the width of the backyard in meters.\n\nFrom the problem, we know two things:\n1. Walking the length 25 times covers 1 kilometer.\n2. Walking the perimeter 10 times also covers 1 kilometer.\n\nWe start by translating these statements into equations.\n\n### Step 1: Equate the first statement\nWalking the length 25 times:\n\\[\n25L = 1000 \\text{ meters}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( L \\):\n\\[\nL = \\frac{1000}{25} = 40 \\text{ meters}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Equate the second statement\nWalking the perimeter 10 times:\nThe perimeter \\( P \\) of the backyard is given by:\n\\[\nP = 2L + 2W\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( L = 40 \\) meters:\n\\[\nP = 2(40) + 2W = 80 + 2W\n\\]\n\nWalking the perimeter 10 times covers 1 kilometer:\n\\[\n10P = 1000 \\text{ meters}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( P = 80 + 2W \\):\n\\[\n10(80 + 2W) = 1000\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\\[\n800 + 20W = 1000\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( W \\):\n\\[\n20W = 1000 - 800\n\\]\n\\[\n20W = 200\n\\]\n\\[\nW = \\frac{200}{20} = 10 \\text{ meters}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the area\nThe area \\( A \\) of the backyard is given by:\n\\[\nA = L \\times W\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( L = 40 \\) meters and \\( W = 10 \\) meters:\n\\[\nA = 40 \\times 10 = 400 \\text{ square meters}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of Mateen's backyard is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{400}\n\\]",
